 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Allows for treatment costing less than $1,500 to be done without prior approval, and more clearly defines the list of \"pre-authorized procedures\" as a floor on treatment as opposed to its current status as a ceiling; allows non-network providers of testing to be compensated at the provider network rate negotiated by the carrier.",
 "title": "Relates to workers' access to treatment",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "S6929",
     "basePrintNoStr": "S6929-2023",
     "printNo": "S6929",
     "session": 2023,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                          VETO MESSAGE - No. 62\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 6929, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend the workers' compensation law, in relation to work-\n      ers' access to treatment\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  enable  treatments  costing  less than $1,500 to be\nperformed without prior approval of  the  Workers'  Compensation  Board,\ndefines  a  non-exhaustive list of pre-authorized procedures, and allows\nnon-network providers of testing  services  to  be  compensated  at  the\nprovider network rate negotiated by the carrier.\n \n  Although  well-intentioned,  this bill dismantles the standard of care\ncontained in the Medical Treatment Guidelines and the expedited delivery\nof quality medical care that the  Guidelines  brought  to  the  workers'\ncompensation  system. The use of evidence-based Medical Treatment Guide-\nlines as the process for injured workers to obtain treatment has created\na uniform standard of evidence-based medical care. The Medical Treatment\nGuidelines are updated when necessary to ensure that they represent  the\nmost  up-to-date  recommendations of the New York State Workers' Compen-\nsation Board Medical Advisory Committee. This  system  was  designed  to\nimprove  the medical outcomes of injured workers and support the workers\nin their return to work,  while  at  the  same  time  reducing  disputes\nconcerning  whether treatment is necessary and appropriate, and reducing\noverall system costs. When a provider seeks to treat an injured  worker,\nany  treatment contained in the relevant Medical Treatment Guidelines is\npre-authorized and paid for, unless  prior  authorization  is  expressly\nrequired.  In  addition, there is currently a clear existing process for\nobtaining treatment that is not specifically authorized. This bill would\neliminate the ability of the payers to deny a request for treatment that\ndeviates from the Medical  Treatment  Guidelines,  resulting  in  payers\nfiling legal objections to medical bills that will require a hearing and\nfurther  delay treatment. This objection and hearing process will result\nin treatment delays for workers, which was the very issue that was elim-\ninated with the establishment of the Medical Treatment Guidelines.\n \n  With respect to treatment requests costing more than $1500, this  bill\nwould require a payer to obtain a second opinion. A second opinion like-\nly  means  that  at  least 90 days will elapse between when treatment is\nrequested and a decision is rendered. Currently, 94% of  prior  authori-\nzation requests are approved without the unnecessary litigation involved\nin  taking  the  testimony of the treating medical providers and payer's\nconsultants.  This  bill  would  disrupt  an  existing  streamlined  and\nmedically  vetted  review  process established in the interest of worker\nsafety.\n \n  Therefore, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
